    sections: [
      { title: 'Ancient Mining Sites & Continuous Operation', content: 'Archaeological excavations across Afghanistan have revealed extensive evidence of ancient mining operations, confirming millennia of continuous mineral extraction. Badakhshan lapis lazuli mines represent the oldest continuously operated mines in human history, with archaeological evidence dating to 4000 BCE. Ancient mining sites preserve tools, processing facilities, and trade artifacts demonstrating sophisticated mining techniques. Excavations reveal evidence of systematic extraction methods, ore processing facilities, and trade networks connecting mining sites to distant markets. The archaeological record confirms continuous operation spanning millennia, with evidence of mining activity across multiple historical periods. Ancient mining sites provide physical proof of Afghanistan\'s mining heritage, complementing written records and demonstrating the exceptional quality that made Afghan minerals sought after throughout history.' },
      { title: 'Trade Artifacts & Global Reach', content: 'Archaeological discoveries of Afghan minerals in distant locations confirm extensive trade networks spanning continents. Lapis lazuli artifacts from Badakhshan found in Egyptian tombs, Mesopotamian sites, and Indus Valley civilizations demonstrate trade relationships spanning 3,000+ kilometers. The presence of Afghan gemstones in royal burials, religious artifacts, and luxury goods confirms their exceptional value and quality. Archaeological evidence includes not only finished artifacts but also raw materials, demonstrating trade in both processed and unprocessed minerals. The global distribution of Afghan mineral artifacts confirms Afghanistan\'s historical role as global supplier of exceptional minerals recognized across civilizations. Archaeological evidence complements written records, providing physical proof of trade relationships and cultural exchange facilitated by mineral commerce.' },
      { title: 'Mining Techniques & Technology', content: 'Archaeological evidence reveals sophisticated mining techniques employed throughout Afghanistan\'s mining heritage. Ancient tools including hammers, chisels, and extraction implements demonstrate advanced understanding of geology and ore extraction. Processing facilities including crushing areas, sorting stations, and storage structures reveal systematic approaches to mineral processing. Evidence of underground mining techniques demonstrates sophisticated engineering knowledge. The archaeological record shows evolution of mining techniques over time, with improvements in extraction efficiency and safety. Traditional techniques passed through generations informed modern exploration, while archaeological evidence provides insights into historical mining practices. The comprehensive archaeological record demonstrates Afghanistan\'s mining expertise accumulated over millennia.' },
      { title: 'Cultural Significance & Heritage Preservation', content: 'Archaeological discoveries reveal the cultural significance of mining throughout Afghanistan\'s history. Mining sites include religious structures, community facilities, and cultural artifacts demonstrating mining\'s central role in local communities. Evidence of trade relationships reveals cultural exchange facilitated by mineral commerce. Archaeological sites preserve evidence of mining traditions, rituals, and community organization around mining activities. The cultural significance extends beyond economic activity, with mining embedded in Afghan cultural heritage. Archaeological preservation efforts ensure that mining heritage continues to inform modern development while honoring historical traditions.' }
    ],
