      { title: 'Ancient Texts & Historical Records', content: 'Ancient texts from multiple civilizations document Afghanistan\'s mining heritage, providing written evidence of millennia of continuous operation. Egyptian records from 2500 BCE mention lapis lazuli trade from Badakhshan, while Mesopotamian texts describe trade routes carrying Afghan gemstones. Ancient Chinese records document lapis lazuli imports along Silk Road precursors, and Indian texts mention Afghan emeralds and rubies. Medieval Islamic texts provide detailed descriptions of Panjshir emerald mining and Badakhshan lapis extraction, documenting techniques and trade relationships. Renaissance European accounts describe ultramarine pigment trade, with artists\' records showing the exceptional value of Afghan lapis. These texts confirm Afghanistan\'s historical role as a global source of exceptional gemstones and demonstrate the continuity of mining operations across millennia.' },
      { title: 'Historical Maps & Trade Routes', content: 'Historical maps from ancient, medieval, and modern periods document trade routes connecting Afghan mining regions to global markets. Ancient maps show routes from Badakhshan through Mesopotamia to Egypt, spanning 3,000+ kilometers. Medieval Islamic maps detail Silk Road connections carrying Afghan minerals to markets across Asia, Europe, and Africa. Renaissance maps show maritime routes connecting Afghan gemstone trade to European markets. Modern geological maps document mining sites identified through comprehensive surveys, building upon historical knowledge. These maps demonstrate Afghanistan\'s central role in global mineral trade networks throughout history, showing how mining regions connected to distant markets through extensive trade routes.' },
      { title: 'Museum Collections & Archaeological Evidence', content: 'Museum collections worldwide preserve Afghan gemstones, demonstrating their historical value and global reach. The British Museum, Louvre, Metropolitan Museum, and collections across Asia preserve lapis lazuli artifacts from Badakhshan, including pieces from Tutankhamun\'s burial. Panjshir emeralds appear in royal collections from India to Europe, demonstrating their historical value. Archaeological sites across Afghanistan preserve evidence of ancient mining operations, with tools, processing facilities, and trade artifacts confirming continuous operation. These collections and archaeological evidence provide physical proof of millennia of mining heritage, complementing written records and demonstrating the exceptional quality that made Afghan minerals sought after throughout history.' }
