    sections: [
      { title: 'Ancient Iron Extraction & Traditional Techniques', content: 'Historical evidence documents iron extraction in Afghanistan dating back to ancient times, with traditional smelting techniques producing iron for local use. Archaeological sites preserve evidence of ancient ironworking, with tools and artifacts demonstrating sophisticated metallurgical knowledge. Traditional techniques passed through generations enabled production of iron for tools, weapons, and construction materials, supporting local economies and regional development. The historical legacy of ironworking demonstrates Afghanistan\'s metallurgical heritage, with traditional knowledge informing modern exploration and development. Ancient iron extraction sites provide evidence of continuous operation, while traditional techniques demonstrate sophisticated understanding of ore processing and metalworking.' },
      { title: 'Hajigak: Asia\\\'s Largest Undeveloped Iron Deposit', content: 'The massive Hajigak iron deposit in Bamyan Province contains an estimated 2.2 billion tons of high-grade iron ore, representing one of Asia\'s largest undeveloped iron reserves. Soviet geological surveys in the 1960s-1980s identified Hajigak as a world-class deposit, with detailed mapping revealing extensive high-grade magnetite ore suitable for direct reduction steelmaking. Modern exploration has confirmed Hajigak\'s exceptional potential, with deposits containing 60%+ iron content suitable for premium steel production. The deposit\'s strategic location and exceptional quality position it as foundation for Afghanistan\'s industrial steel future, with potential to establish Central Asia\'s largest integrated steel complex. Historical mining in the region provides context for modern development, while traditional knowledge informs exploration and development strategies.' },
      { title: 'Modern Steel Production Vision', content: 'Modern development plans envision integrated steel production facilities transforming Hajigak iron ore into finished steel products, capturing value throughout supply chains. Integrated steel complexes will produce construction steel, reinforcing bars, and structural steel supporting national infrastructure development. Value-added processing maximizes economic benefit while creating skilled employment, continuing the historical tradition of metallurgical excellence. Modern steel production builds upon historical ironworking knowledge, combining traditional expertise with world-class technology. The vision includes Afghanistan as Central Asia\'s steel manufacturing hub, with production supporting regional infrastructure development while generating substantial revenue and employment.' }
    ],
