    sections: [
      { title: 'Arabic Geographical Documentation: Istakhri (951), Ibn Haukal (978), al-Biruni (1050)', content: 'Afghanistan\'s ruby mines were documented in Arabic writings by early travelers dating back to the 10th-14th centuries. Istakhri documented Jegdalek ruby deposits in 951 AD in his geographical work, providing earliest written gemological record. Ibn Haukal documented Afghan gemstone deposits including rubies and lapis lazuli in 978 AD in travel accounts. Persian scholar al-Biruni (d. ca 1050 AD) included detailed gemological information about Afghan gemstones including rubies in mineralogical works. Teifaschi documented Afghan gemstone deposits in Arabic writings in 1240 AD, providing detailed descriptions of quality characteristics. Ibn Battuta documented gemstone deposits during travels from 1325-1354 AD, describing quality and market value of Afghan materials including rubies. These historical Arabic geographical works provide foundation for understanding Jegdalek ruby mining history spanning over 1,000 years. Source: Istakhri (951 AD); Ibn Haukal (978 AD); al-Biruni (1050 AD); Teifaschi (1240 AD); Ibn Battuta (1325-1354 AD).' },
      { title: 'Kublai Khan Ruby Sales: Muhammadan Merchants (1200s)', content: 'During the 1200s, wealthy Muhammadan merchants sold rubies from Jegdalek to Kublai Khan and other historical figures, demonstrating quality recognition and trade networks. These merchants could reportedly distinguish between ruby and spinel, demonstrating sophisticated gemological knowledge. The sales to Kublai Khan, who ruled the Mongol Empire from 1260-1294, demonstrate the exceptional quality and value of Jegdalek rubies, as they reached one of the most powerful rulers of the medieval world. The trade relationship demonstrates extensive trade networks connecting Afghanistan to Central Asia and China during the Mongol period. The ability of merchants to distinguish ruby from spinel indicates advanced gemological knowledge and quality assessment capabilities. Source: Historical trade records; GIA (2000).' },
      { title: 'Geological Formation: Corundum-Bearing Marble Deposits', content: 'The Jegdalek deposit is hosted by elongate beds of corundum-bearing marble, with ruby formation occurring through metamorphic processes. While some facet-grade ruby has been recovered from Jegdalek, most material consists of semitransparent pink sapphire suitable for cabochon or carving. The geological formation creates characteristic inclusions including rutile needles and zircon crystals, with refractive indices of 1.762-1.770 and specific gravity of 3.97-4.05. The deposit characteristics determine the cutting and utilization methods, with cabochon cutting preserving material with inclusions. The geological formation provides foundation for understanding ruby occurrence and extraction methods. Source: GIA (2000); Geological analysis.' },
      { title: 'Modern Mining Operations: Soviet Period (1979) & Current Tribal Mining', content: 'For most of the last 100 years, the mines were owned and operated sporadically by the Afghan government. Following the 1979 Soviet invasion, the Soviets operated the mines for five to six months before ceasing operations. Today, the mines are exploited solely by local tribal people year-round, with artisanal mining methods continuing traditional extraction practices. The modern mining operations demonstrate continuity of 700+ year mining tradition, with local communities maintaining knowledge and techniques passed through generations. The year-round operation contrasts with many other mining sites that operate seasonally, demonstrating accessibility and consistent production capacity. Source: GIA (2000); Mining operation records.' }
    ],
