    sections: [
      { title: 'Periplus Maris Erythraei: 1st Century Merchant Guide Documentation', content: 'The first-century merchant guide Periplus Maris Erythraei documents Kushan Empire (ca. 50-200 CE) engagement in western Indian Ocean maritime trade networks. The Periplus provides detailed information about trade routes, ports, and commodities, including Afghan products reaching maritime markets. The guide demonstrates Afghanistan\'s connection to Red Sea and Persian Gulf maritime networks during the 1st century CE, with commodities from Afghanistan holding prominent place in western Indian Ocean trade. The Periplus represents earliest written documentation of Afghanistan\'s maritime trade connections, providing foundation for understanding ancient trade networks. Source: Periplus Maris Erythraei (1st century CE); Kushan Empire maritime networks.' },
      { title: 'Palmyra Archaeological Evidence: 2nd Century Material Culture', content: 'Second-century archaeological material from Palmyra provides evidence of Afghanistan\'s maritime trade connections during the 2nd century CE. Palmyra, located in modern Syria, served as important trading center connecting Mediterranean and Asian trade routes. Archaeological finds from Palmyra demonstrate Afghan commodities reaching Mediterranean markets through maritime and overland routes. The Palmyra evidence confirms continued maritime trade connections during the 2nd century, with Afghan products integrated into broader trade networks. The archaeological material provides physical evidence complementing written documentation, demonstrating material culture exchange. Source: Palmyra archaeological evidence (2nd century CE); Archaeological documentation.' },
      { title: 'Kushan Empire Maritime Networks: ca. 50-200 CE Commercial Reach', content: 'The Kushan Empire (ca. 50-200 CE) engaged in maritime networks connecting Afghanistan to the Indian Ocean, Red Sea, and Persian Gulf. The Kushan Empire controlled significant portions of Afghanistan and Central Asia, enabling integration into maritime trade networks. Between the 1st and 2nd centuries CE, commodities from Afghanistan held prominent place in western Indian Ocean maritime trade, demonstrating the empire\'s commercial reach. The maritime connections enabled Afghan products to reach distant markets, with trade networks spanning from Indian Ocean to Mediterranean. The Kushan period represents peak of Afghanistan\'s ancient maritime trade connections, with extensive commercial networks. Source: Kushan Empire (ca. 50-200 CE); Maritime network documentation; Trade route records.' },
      { title: 'Historical Trade Routes: 6th Century BCE-19th Century Continuity', content: 'Historical routes included the Uttarapath, western routes to the Mediterranean, the Wakhan Corridor route, the Lapis Lazuli route, and passages through Sulaiman mountains. These routes spanned from the 6th century BCE through the 19th century, facilitating Indian trade and contributing to prosperity in both regions. The Uttarapath represented northern route connecting India to Central Asia through Afghanistan. The Lapis Lazuli route specifically connected Badakhshan lapis lazuli mines to maritime markets. The Wakhan Corridor route provided connection between Central Asia and South Asia. The routes demonstrate long-term continuity of trade connections, with maritime links maintained across centuries. Source: Historical route documentation; Trade route records (6th century BCE-19th century).' }
    ],
