    sections: [
      { title: 'Classical Sources: Pedanius Dioscorides & Pliny the Elder (1st Century)', content: 'Pedanius Dioscorides and Pliny the Elder, both from the 1st century, wrote about lapis lazuli in their works, providing earliest documented references to Afghan lapis lazuli from classical sources. Dioscorides, a Greek physician and pharmacologist, documented lapis lazuli in his work "De Materia Medica," describing its medicinal and artistic uses. Pliny the Elder, a Roman naturalist, documented lapis lazuli in his "Natural History," providing detailed descriptions of the mineral\'s properties and sources. The 1st century references represent earliest written documentation of Afghan lapis lazuli in classical literature, demonstrating the mineral\'s recognition in ancient Mediterranean world. Source: Dioscorides "De Materia Medica" (1st century); Pliny the Elder "Natural History" (1st century).' },
      { title: 'Marco Polo: Medieval Documentation (1271)', content: 'Marco Polo referred to the area\'s lapis mines in 1271 during his travels, documenting lapis lazuli sources in his travel accounts. Marco Polo\'s references provide medieval documentation of Afghan lapis lazuli mines, connecting classical knowledge with medieval trade networks. The 1271 reference demonstrates continued recognition of Afghan lapis lazuli sources during medieval period, with trade routes maintaining connections to Mediterranean markets. Marco Polo\'s documentation provides evidence of lapis lazuli trade during medieval period, demonstrating long-term commercial connections. Source: Marco Polo travel accounts (1271); Medieval trade documentation.' },
      { title: 'Georgina Herrmann: Modern Research Establishing Badakhshan Origins (1968)', content: 'Georgina Herrmann (1968) produced a well-documented work speculating on the origins of lapis lazuli, concluding it came from the mines of Badakhshan in Afghanistan. Herrmann\'s research established the connection between Afghan lapis and ancient civilizations, providing modern scholarly foundation for understanding lapis lazuli sources. The 1968 work represents comprehensive modern documentation of lapis lazuli origins, synthesizing archaeological and historical evidence. Herrmann\'s conclusion that Badakhshan was the source for ancient lapis lazuli has been supported by subsequent research, demonstrating accuracy of her analysis. Source: Georgina Herrmann (1968); Modern lapis lazuli research.' },
      { title: 'Tutankhamun Mask: Howard Carter Excavation (1925) & 1323 BCE Artifact', content: 'The most renowned artifact is Tutankhamun\'s golden funerary mask containing lapis lazuli inlays from Sar-e-Sang, excavated by Howard Carter in 1925. The mask dates to 1323 BCE (Tutankhamun\'s death), with lapis lazuli inlays demonstrating use of Afghan lapis in ancient Egyptian royal artifacts. Howard Carter\'s 1925 excavation of Tutankhamun\'s tomb revealed the mask, providing direct archaeological evidence of Afghan lapis lazuli use in ancient Egypt. The mask represents one of the most famous archaeological discoveries, demonstrating the value placed on Afghan lapis lazuli in ancient royal contexts. Source: Howard Carter excavation (1925); Tutankhamun mask documentation; Archaeological evidence.' },
      { title: 'Mesopotamia & Egypt: Ancient Trade Connections (4,000+ Years)', content: 'Mesopotamia imported lapis lazuli from Afghanistan more than 4,000 years ago, establishing commercial relations between ancient Iraq and Afghanistan across 2,500 km. The material appeared particularly important during the 3rd Dynasty at Ur (c. 2112-2004 BCE), where excavations revealed cylinder seals, necklaces, and statuettes fashioned from lapis-lazuli. Egypt also imported the stone during predynastic and early dynastic periods (c. 6000-2686 BCE), demonstrating long-term trade connections. The 2,500 km distance demonstrates extensive trade networks connecting Mesopotamia to Badakhshan mines. Source: Mesopotamian archaeological evidence; 3rd Dynasty at Ur documentation; Egyptian archaeological evidence; Trade route records.' }
    ],
