    sections: [
      { title: 'Metropolitan Museum of Art: 195 Objects & Hidden Treasures Exhibition (2009)', content: 'The Metropolitan Museum of Art holds 195 objects related to Afghanistan in its collection, demonstrating the museum\'s extensive holdings of Afghan artifacts. The Met hosted the major exhibition "Afghanistan: Hidden Treasures from the National Museum, Kabul" from June to September 2009, featuring spectacular artifacts including gold vessels from Tepe Fullol, turquoise-inlaid gold jewelry from Tillya Tepe, and Roman glass and ivories from Begram. The exhibition showcased Afghanistan\'s rich cultural heritage, with artifacts spanning from ancient periods through medieval times. The 195 objects in the Met\'s collection represent diverse materials including gold, silver, gemstones, glass, and ivory, demonstrating the range of Afghan artistic production. Source: Metropolitan Museum of Art collection; Exhibition documentation (June-September 2009).' },
      { title: 'National Museum of Afghanistan: 100,000+ Original Collection, 50,000-80,000 Current', content: 'The National Museum of Afghanistan in Kabul originally held over 100,000 items but was severely damaged during civil war, losing 70% of its collection. Current holdings are estimated at 50,000-80,000 objects, representing significant reduction from original collection. The museum\'s collection includes artifacts from archaeological sites across Afghanistan, with minerals and gemstones featured in jewelry, decorative objects, and ceremonial items. The civil war damage represents significant loss of cultural heritage, with efforts to preserve remaining collections. Source: National Museum of Afghanistan; Collection documentation.' },
      { title: 'Tillya Tepe Bactrian Gold: Viktor Sarianidi Excavation (1978) & 20,600 Artifacts', content: 'The Tillya Tepe Bactrian Gold hoard, excavated by Viktor Sarianidi in 1978, contains approximately 20,600 gold, silver, and ivory artifacts dated to 1st century BCE-CE. The hoard was discovered in six burial mounds containing rich jewelry, necklaces set with semi-precious stones, belts, medallions, and crowns. The gold jewelry features lapis lazuli, turquoise, and other gemstones, demonstrating use of Afghan minerals in ancient artifacts. The hoard represents one of the most significant archaeological gold discoveries in Central Asia, providing insight into ancient gold mining and trade. Source: Viktor Sarianidi excavation (1978); Tillya Tepe documentation; Bactrian Gold hoard.' },
      { title: 'Tutankhamun Mask: Howard Carter (1925) & Lapis Lazuli from Sar-e Sang', content: 'Tutankhamun\'s golden funerary mask, excavated by Howard Carter in 1925, contains lapis lazuli inlays from Sar-e Sang, Badakhshan, demonstrating Afghan lapis in ancient Egyptian royal artifacts. The mask dates to 1323 BCE (Tutankhamun\'s death), with lapis lazuli inlays in the headdress stripes (nemes), eyelids, eyebrows, and decorative elements. The mask represents one of the most famous archaeological discoveries, demonstrating the value placed on Afghan lapis lazuli in ancient royal contexts. The mask is preserved in the Egyptian Museum in Cairo, representing Afghan lapis in world museum collections. Source: Howard Carter excavation (1925); Tutankhamun mask documentation; Egyptian Museum Cairo.' },
      { title: 'Corsi Collection Oxford: Lapis Lazuli Specimens from Kokcha Valley', content: 'The Oxford University Museum of Natural History\'s Corsi Collection includes lapis lazuli specimens from Kokcha Valley in Badakhshan, demonstrating Afghan lapis in European museum collections. The Corsi Collection represents one of the world\'s most important collections of decorative stones, with lapis lazuli specimens documenting the mineral\'s characteristics and sources. The collection preserves historical lapis lazuli specimens, providing foundation for gemological research. Source: Corsi Collection; Oxford University Museum of Natural History.' }
    ],
